Scope and interface
-------------------

The module implements the default QCD calculation for exactly two incoming
legs, ordinary quark/gluon colour lines and a valid selected FKS Born flow.
It does not search shower histories, rearrange resonance records or handle
colour junctions and general electroweak/BSM shower histories. Unlike the
former runtime interface, it does not test history admissibility in Pythia.

  use mcatnlo_delta_scales, only: dp, delta_scale_matrices, delta_ok
  ! real(dp) p(0:3,nreal), scales(0:nreal,0:nreal), masses(0:nreal,0:nreal)
  ! integer id_real(nreal), born_col(2,nreal-1), status
  ! logical dead(0:nreal,0:nreal)
  call delta_scale_matrices(nreal, emitted, p, id_real, born_col, &
                           charm_mass, bottom_mass, scales, masses, dead, status)

Momenta have components (E,px,py,pz), with positive incoming energies.
Incoming legs occupy positions 1 and 2. Born labels are real labels with
the emitted leg deleted. Output indices use the original real labels;
index 0, diagonal entries, the emitted row/column and absent dipoles have
scale/mass -1 and dead=true. Scales are pT in GeV, not squared pT. Both
directions of a dipole are evaluated separately. Double gluon connections
occupy one matrix cell per direction; MG5 handles their multiplicity.

Use default-kind logical arrays, not logical*1. Status 0 (delta_ok) means
success, 1 (delta_bad_input) invalid input/unsupported colour structure,
and 2 (delta_numerical) an undefined numerical expression. MG5 stops with
the FKS configuration on a nonzero status; there is no runtime fallback.

charm_mass and bottom_mass are shower particle-data masses in the original
formulas. MG5 supplies get_mass_from_id(4) and get_mass_from_id(5), so both
follow the model restriction. In a five-flavour model with massless charm
and bottom, both lookups return zero even when the corresponding mass
parameters have been removed. Massive-charm models now use their charm
mass; the former runtime wrapper always passed zero for charm. The
bottom-mass prescription is unchanged. The old wrapper serialized masses
using C++ default stream precision;
unusually precise model masses can therefore yield small differences.
The separate masses supplied to MG5's Sudakov tables are unchanged.

Initial-state scales use the opposite incoming beam as kinematic recoiler,
also for initial-final colour connections. The sentinel values are kept:
raw 1e-6 is live, raw 1e-5 is dead, raw 1e10 is stored as 0 and dead, and
zero is dead. MG5 then imposes its starting-scale veto and table bounds.
The low-level pythia_pt_lund routine exposes the raw scalar calculation.

Supplied reference validation
-----------------------------

The independent tests recorded on 2026-09-14 used GNU Fortran/C++ 13.3.0
with Fortran runtime checks, Pythia 8.313 and the old MG5 runtime wrapper.
56,000 finite scalar comparisons agreed exactly in that build. 3,500 full
interface comparisons covered seven processes and 11,000 populated dipole
ends, including 1,253 dead ends. All flags and populated/absent entries
agreed; the largest abs(Fortran-Pythia)/max(1,abs(Pythia)) difference was
4.28e-11. These are scale/interface comparisons, not integrated event or
cross-section validation. See docs/mcatnlo_delta_validation.md in the MG5
source distribution for the integration context and validation history.
